WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.230
Good morning John, I got to do
something very weird this year.

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:07.740
I got put into a room with 4 other
dorks, and we together judged the

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.180
Nikon small world photography
competition.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:11.340
Photomicrography

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.340
I should say.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.340
Not photography.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:14.929
Beautiful pictures, using microscopes.

00:00:14.929 --> 00:00:17.570
We talked about technique, how
clever and skilled someone had

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.390
to be to capture the image. We
talked about beauty, how did the

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.390
images make us feel. And we
talked about story, what did the

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.650
image tell us about the world? It was really fun, and happened

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.560
a while ago, but now finally the
results have been released

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.560
to the world.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.270
So I get to give you
right now the top 10 best

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:39.250
microscopic images of 2021 as
judged by 4 experts and me.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.470
Starting out with number 10, this is
a butterfly wing. There were a

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.220
lot of butterfly wings and that
makes sense because they are

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.580
absolutely amazing.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:46.870
Something about
the composition of this one,

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:50.739
with that huge brown vein and that
one misplaced scale shot this

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:53.410
one to the top 10. Number 9 is one that I
personally fought for.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.809
This is a daphnia, it’s a tiny crustaceon 
and they are not chill.

00:00:56.809 --> 00:00:57.809
I have followed them with

00:00:57.809 --> 00:00:59.789
my own microscope and getting
an image this sharp with this

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:01.610
much color contrast is amazing.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:02.879
But in addition to that, this little

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:07.280
water flea is covered in, I think
vorticellas, single celled filter feeder

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.929
that eats some of the same stuff
as the daphnia.

00:01:09.929 --> 00:01:10.929
And then up in there,

00:01:10.929 --> 00:01:13.689
those little blue dots-- those
are baby daphnia.

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:16.880
Number 8 is a cross section of a
mouse intestine, which we all liked

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.389
because it’s a reminder that a
lot of the diagrams in anatomy

00:01:19.389 --> 00:01:21.240
textbooks are simplifications.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.020
The way fluorescence microscopy is used

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.189
here really lets you see how the
surface area is maximized in the intestine

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:28.920
which increases nutrient absorption.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.370
Number 7 is the head of a tick, we

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:33.350
were all just really pleased when
we saw beautiful images of things

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.669
that we generally find disgusting.

00:01:35.669 --> 00:01:39.079
Number 6, these are the blood vessels of a mouse’s brain.

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:41.569
And as impressive
and fascinating as it is, I think I

00:01:41.569 --> 00:01:46.159
primarily liked this one as a piece
of art that I would absolutely hang on my wall.

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:49.789
Number 5 again, we love the gross stuff. This is the proboscis of a house fly,

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:53.599
and maybe you didn’t want to see
that but also it’s really amazing!

00:01:53.599 --> 00:01:55.189
And the more we looked
at it the more we loved it.

00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:58.239
In fourth, we have a single sensory
neuron of an embryonic rat.

00:01:58.239 --> 00:02:02.849
Again, we’re used to these oversimplified diagrams of neurons with a couple dozen branches

00:02:02.849 --> 00:02:07.369
at max, but neurons can have thousands
of dendrites that connect to tens

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:11.250
of thousands of other cells. And now it’s time for our top 3.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.640
In 3rd place, this leg claw of a louse.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.410
We really did hit as many annoying bugs as we could.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.020
I like this one because
the tracheae which are the holes that

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.569
they breathe air into their bodies through,
they’re like the wormholes that Bill and

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:26.840
Ted would fly through in Spacetime.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:30.310
Like the whole thing just looks like

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:33.450
a galaxy, but it’s a louse. Speaking of galaxies, in second place

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.980
this one is a bunch of neurons
that were put on two sides of a barrier

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:41.239
with tiny channels in it, and then because
the nuclei couldn’t fit through those channels,

00:02:41.239 --> 00:02:43.510
the axons of the neurons did.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:45.090
And they meshed together into this

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.790
image that absolutely looks like some
kind of science fiction megastructure.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:53.810
And finally, what beautiful, weird,
otherworldly thing did we choose for our

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.680
number 1 image of the year?

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.261
Was it something
maybe you’d never seen before, some

00:02:58.261 --> 00:03:03.499
new discovery, some tiny organism
that only scientists pay attention to?

00:03:03.499 --> 00:03:04.499
No.

00:03:04.499 --> 00:03:05.499
It is a leaf.

00:03:05.499 --> 00:03:08.930
I believe it is the underside
of a leaf of the southern live oak, shown

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.390
in a way that completely reshapes my
understanding of something so simple.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.900
This image, which is the result of stacking
over 200 different images focusing

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:21.620
on different planes, shows 3 different
vital leaf structures: stomata in purple,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.519
trichomes in white, and veins in blue.

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:28.069
It’s an oak leaf, alive and complex and mysterious like I never imagined.

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:29.719
Thanks so much to Nikon Small World

00:03:29.719 --> 00:03:32.569
for putting this together and providing
those images for this video.

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:35.829
There were so many other amazing
images which you can go see if you

00:03:35.829 --> 00:03:38.450
just go to nikonsmallworld.com. I think there were something like 100

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.530
total images that we have some
kind of distinction.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.950
It was a wonderful way to spend 2 days, thanks to all of the other judges and everybody

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.739
who submitted their work to the
competition.

00:03:45.739 --> 00:03:51.310
It is a reminder of how amazing our world is, and
how amazing our tools for observing it are,

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:55.629
and a reminder of how amazing people are who
observe this world in all of the different

00:03:55.629 --> 00:03:56.749
ways we observe it.

00:03:56.749 --> 00:03:58.049
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

